<DocsLayout
  title="Viewport"
  subtitle="Canvas drawing tools and Three.js rendering share core geometry while keeping editing and playback state explicit."
  description="2D editor integration, implemented tools, 3D rendering, toolpath playback, workers, units, and lifecycle."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="status" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Viewport components</h2>
    <p><code>apps/desktop/src/viewport/index.ts</code> exports <code>Editor2D</code>, <code>View3D</code>, their prop types, available tool identifiers, and <code>solidsFromProfiles</code>. Both components fill their parent: provide a container with a non-zero width and height. Core CAD entities and CAM programs are the shared data model.</p>
    <p>The 2D editor renders to Canvas 2D and maintains its own selection, camera, and command history. The 3D viewer uses Three.js, orbit and transform controls, asynchronous mesh jobs, and program playback. Rendering a path or solid is independent of saving it or verifying a manufacturing operation.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="paths" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Integration contract</h2>
    <p><code>Editor2DProps</code> requires <code>drawing</code>, <code>onChange</code>, and <code>selectedTool</code>. Feed emitted drawings back into the prop. The component recognizes its own emitted document objects; replacing the document externally loads the new drawing and resets editor state.</p>
    <DocCode language="tsx" title="Controlled 2D editor">
{`import { createSignal } from "solid-js"
import { Editor2D, type ToolId } from "./viewport/index.ts"

const [drawing, setDrawing] = createSignal(initialDrawing)
const [tool, setTool] = createSignal<ToolId>("select")

<Editor2D drawing={drawing()} onChange={setDrawing} selectedTool={tool()} />`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>Selection excludes locked entities and entities on locked layers. Invisible entities and hidden layers are excluded from the rendered spatial index. New entities receive document-unique identifiers and are assigned to a visible, unlocked layer when one is available.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="tools" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Drawing tools and gestures</h2>
    <p>The tool registry has factories for select, line, rectangle, circle, ellipse, polyline, arc, text, dimension, move, rotate, scale, mirror, offset, trim, and fillet. <code>AVAILABLE_TOOLS</code> is derived from those registered factories. <code>startTool</code> additionally recognizes <code>chamfer</code> through the fillet tool factory, but chamfer is not advertised in that array. Consumers should use the actual registry to decide which tools to enable.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Wheel or pinch zooms; Space-drag, middle-drag, and right-drag pan.</li>
      <li><code>F</code> fits the indexed drawing. Delete or Backspace removes the unlocked selection.</li>
      <li><code>Cmd/Ctrl+Z</code> undoes; <code>Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+Z</code> or <code>Cmd/Ctrl+Y</code> redoes.</li>
      <li>Enter commits a polyline. Escape is delivered to the active tool to cancel its current gesture.</li>
      <li>With text active, type characters or Backspace before placement; the initial text is <code>Text</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Pointer positions are converted to world coordinates before tool dispatch. Snapping combines the spatial index, geometric candidates, and a camera-dependent grid; the selection hit tolerance corresponds to eight screen pixels. Tool machines produce commands and previews rather than writing files or calling the API.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="view3d" class="doc-section">
    <h2>3D rendering and playback</h2>
    <p><code>View3D</code> accepts drawings, solids, optional profile extrusion, a <code>cam.Program</code>, stock dimensions, and tool definitions. It supports perspective, top, bottom, front, back, left, right, isometric, and four-view presets. Display modes are shaded, shaded with edges, wireframe, and x-ray. Lighting, material properties, clipping sections, grid, axes, and origin are configurable through props.</p>
    <p>Control playback with <code>playing</code>, <code>playbackTime</code> in seconds, <code>playbackRate</code>, and <code>onPlaybackTimeChange</code>. Playback expands drilling cycles, tessellates circular/helical motion, preserves dwell time, and draws rapid moves distinctly. Tool maps and an optional default cutter supply cutter, shank, and holder geometry; assumed geometry is visually distinguished.</p>
    <p>Selection callbacks report a solid index. Transform controls emit position, rotation, and scale through <code>onTransformCommit</code>; callers decide how to persist that transform. Toolpath overlays can show points and labels, point-kind filters, arc centers, arc radii, and stock transparency.</p>
    <DocCallout type="note" title="Playback is a visual representation">
      <p>The viewer animates the supplied program and draws stock and tools. It does not perform material removal. Collision and machine-envelope checks belong to the CAM verification modules; viewing a program does not constitute machine approval.</p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="units" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Coordinates and units</h2>
    <p>Core drawing coordinates and stock sizes use millimetres. Drawing angles and Three.js rotation components use radians; do not apply that convention to CAM rotary-axis values or feature inputs that explicitly use degrees. <code>viewport/units.ts</code> contains conversion helpers for displayed millimetres and inches. Convert at input/output boundaries so snapping, geometry, and toolpath motion retain a consistent unit system.</p>
    <p>The 2D camera maps world coordinates to CSS pixels; backing canvas dimensions incorporate device pixel ratio. Resizing updates the camera and backing buffer through <code>ResizeObserver</code>. Camera scale determines grid spacing and screen-space hit tolerances.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="boundaries" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Resources and lifecycle</h2>
    <p>The 2D component cancels queued animation frames and disconnects its resize observer on unmount. The 3D component releases resize and visibility listeners, orbit and transform controls, mesh workers, animation scheduling, geometries, materials, sprites, tool meshes, and the WebGL renderer.</p>
    <p>A frame scheduler coalesces invalidations and requests further frames while camera damping or playback needs them. Visibility changes suspend work. This is a scheduling policy, not a guaranteed frame-rate budget for every model and device.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="module-tree" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Module map</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><code>editor2d/editor2d.tsx</code>: controlled component, pointer dispatch, camera, selection, history, and canvas painting.</li>
      <li><code>editor2d/tools/</code>: gesture state machines and command construction.</li>
      <li><code>editor2d/camera.ts</code>, <code>render.ts</code>, and <code>snap-pipeline.ts</code>: coordinate transforms, entity rendering, picking, and snap orchestration.</li>
      <li><code>spatial/grid.ts</code>: drawing spatial index.</li>
      <li><code>view3d/view3d.tsx</code>: scene composition, camera controls, selection, transforms, stock, and tools.</li>
      <li><code>view3d/playback.ts</code>, <code>toolpath-overlays.ts</code>, and <code>modeling.ts</code>: motion timeline, overlay data, and profile-to-solid conversion.</li>
      <li><code>view3d/mesh-jobs.ts</code> and <code>mesh-worker.ts</code>: background geometry construction and job ownership.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="tool-ids" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Tool availability</h2>
    <p><code>ToolId</code> describes the larger tool vocabulary; <code>AVAILABLE_TOOLS</code> describes the registered subset. For interactive tools, use <code>startTool(id, lookup, context, text)</code>. Selection lookup queries candidate entities; context supplies the current selection and entity access; text supplies the annotation contents.</p>
    <p>A missing factory returns <code>undefined</code>. Handle that explicitly rather than presenting a disabled or unimplemented tool as an active editing mode. Tools that modify existing geometry require current selection and entity context; invoking a factory without those dependencies is insufficient for a useful edit.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="module-roles" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Meshing and verification</h2>
    <p>For solids with an estimated vertex count above 50,000 and browser Worker support, the viewer queues mesh construction in a module worker. Smaller solids mesh synchronously. The mesh queue handles completion, stale jobs, worker errors, and cleanup. The threshold is an estimate used for scheduling, not a documented model-size limit.</p>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="Viewport logic checks, from the repository root">
{`bun test apps/desktop/src/viewport
bun run --filter @cadcamfun/desktop typecheck`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>Logic tests cover rendering helpers, tool gestures, spatial indexing, playback, and overlays. Browser/WebGL behavior also depends on the graphics environment; inspect actual viewport interaction when changing camera controls, scene lifecycle, or rendering.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="playback-example" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Controlled playback example</h2>
    <p>A parent can own transport controls without duplicating the viewer's motion timeline. This component accepts an already constructed CAM program and controls time, play state, speed, and the viewport preset. Its import paths assume a file directly under <code>apps/desktop/src</code>.</p>
    <DocCode language="tsx" title="Program preview with external transport controls">
{`import { createSignal } from "solid-js"
import { cam } from "@cadcamfun/core"
import { View3D } from "./viewport/index.ts"

export function ProgramPreview(props: { program: cam.Program }) {
  const [seconds, setSeconds] = createSignal(0)
  const [playing, setPlaying] = createSignal(false)
  return <div style={{ height: "600px" }}>
    <button onClick={() => setPlaying(value => !value)}>Play / pause</button>
    <button onClick={() => { setSeconds(0); setPlaying(true) }}>Restart</button>
    <View3D
      program={props.program}
      preset="isometric"
      playing={playing()}
      playbackTime={seconds()}
      playbackRate={1}
      onPlaybackTimeChange={setSeconds}
      stock={{ origin: { x: 0, y: 0, z: -10 }, size: { x: 100, y: 80, z: 10 } }}
    />
  </div>
}`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>The playback builder starts at the origin, expands cycles into moves, and calculates durations from travel length and feed. Its default rapid speed is 5,000 mm/min and default arc tessellation tolerance is 0.1 mm. These are preview assumptions, not measured machine acceleration or controller timing. Dwell commands retain their stated seconds; comments and orientation-only entries do not add linear travel.</p>
    <p>Tool colors are assigned by each tool identifier's first occurrence and retained across its paths. Supply a tool map/default tool for assembly geometry; a colored path alone does not establish the cutter or holder dimensions used by verification.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="editing-recipes" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Editing recipes and current tool limits</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Gesture and preconditions</th><th>Current behavior</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Offset</td><td>Select a supported entity, then drag; Shift chooses the entity under the cursor instead.</td><td>Lines/polylines/rectangles produce a polyline result. Circle and arc implementations translate the center while retaining radius; this tool is not a general concentric-offset operation for curves.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Trim / extend</td><td>Select source and boundary entities first, activate Trim, and click the target. Alt selects extend mode.</td><td>The implementation searches the other selected entities for intersections. It changes lines and polylines; unsupported target kinds or missing intersections produce no edit.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fillet / chamfer</td><td>Pick two intersecting lines. Shift switches to chamfer.</td><td>The shared tool uses an initial radius of 5 mm and replaces the pair with trimmed lines and a connecting polyline. It is not an arbitrary solid-edge fillet.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Transform a solid</td><td>Provide <code>onTransformCommit</code>, select a mesh, then activate translate/rotate/scale.</td><td>The callback reports the solid index and transform values. Picking and transform gizmos are disabled in four-view mode.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>These details come from the gesture implementations rather than their palette labels. Inspect the preview before committing and use Undo if the result differs from the intended geometry. A tool returning no command can mean its geometric prerequisites were not satisfied, not that the editor lost the pointer event.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="rendering-failures" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Rendering and meshing failures</h2>
    <p>The background mesh queue admits at most 128 pending jobs by default, runs one at a time, and has a default 15-second timeout per job. Resetting a scene terminates the worker and clears queued work; stale responses are ignored. Invalid worker responses, message decoding errors, timeouts, and queue capacity failures report errors through the queue callback.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Empty canvas with no geometry</td><td>Give the parent a non-zero size, check entity/layer visibility, and fit the view.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Nothing can be selected</td><td>Check entity/layer locks in 2D; check four-view mode and transform state in 3D.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mesh timeout or full queue</td><td>Reduce scene complexity or the number of simultaneously submitted solids and inspect the worker error. A failed batch is cleared rather than installed partially as a completed job.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Playback time seems wrong</td><td>Check program feeds, dwell moves, rapid-speed assumptions, and whether a changed program reset the parent time.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>WebGL is unavailable</td><td>Use a supported graphics environment and inspect the component's unavailable-renderer state; logic tests cannot prove GPU availability.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Relevant tests include <code>editor2d/tools/offset.test.ts</code>, <code>annotation-transform.test.ts</code>, <code>spatial/grid.test.ts</code>, <code>view3d/playback.test.ts</code>, and <code>toolpath-overlays.test.ts</code>. The mesh queue implementation is <code>view3d/mesh-jobs.ts</code>.</p>
  </section>

